“Idea 137” was just a made-up placeholder, like saying “variable X” or “function foo()” in programming.

That said… 137 itself is a fascinating number because it shows up in physics.

The inverse of the fine-structure constant is approximately:

\alpha^{-1} \approx 137.036

The fine-structure constant, \alpha, measures the strength of the electromagnetic interaction. It’s dimensionless, meaning it doesn’t depend on human units like meters or seconds. Physicists such as Richard Feynman famously remarked that it’s one of the deepest unexplained numbers in physics—we can measure it extremely precisely, but the Standard Model doesn’t explain why it has that value.

So purely by coincidence, I happened to pick a number that has its own history.

